# The catalogue endpoint, as a REST client sends it (Rider, or VS Code's REST Client extension).
#
# Before running these: `dotnet run` in Development loads appsettings.Local.json and the developer's
# user secrets, so identity is configured and every request below is answered 401. That is correct
# behaviour - the endpoint wants a token. Either run the application the way a machine caller meets an
# unconfigured deployment, which leaves the catalogue open exactly as the store page is:
#
#   ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production ASPNETCORE_URLS=http://127.0.0.1:5080 \
#     dotnet run --no-launch-profile --project src/Host/CoreRentalNet.Host
#
# or keep Development and set @token to a machine-to-machine access token carrying read:catalog.

@host = http://127.0.0.1:5080
@token = paste-a-token-here

### The whole catalogue - 62 products
GET {{host}}/api/catalog
Accept: application/json

### One category: chair, desk or accessory
GET {{host}}/api/catalog?category=desk

### One subcategory: beanbag, coffee, lamp, monitor or plant
GET {{host}}/api/catalog?subCategory=monitor

### A name search - a term matches the product's name only
GET {{host}}/api/catalog?search=teak

### No match is an empty list, not a 404
GET {{host}}/api/catalog?search=zzzzzzzz

### An unknown filter is refused with the values that would have worked
GET {{host}}/api/catalog?category=sofa

### With identity configured, the read:catalog permission travels on a bearer token
GET {{host}}/api/catalog
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### The machine-readable description of everything above (Development only)
GET {{host}}/openapi/v1.json
